(5528424, 796133, 103142, '<td valign="top">already commented that I loved the title--Loved the reds and the greys. It''s too bad that the DOF had to be so shallow. There was such a large fuzzy area that it was distracting. The rest was awesome!</td>', 'already commented that I loved the title--Loved the reds and the greys. It''s too bad that the DOF had to be so shallow. There was such a large fuzzy area that it was distracting. The rest was awesome!', '2009-06-15 01:29:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(5528435, 796246, 103142, '<td valign="top">Liked the shot quite a bit, but I think there''s too much rose in it. If it wasn''t for the title, I wouldn''t have noticed the ants. Could you have cropped it portrait and brought the focus in a little more on the bugs, but still have the feel and idea of the rose?</td>', 'Liked the shot quite a bit, but I think there''s too much rose in it. If it wasn''t for the title, I wouldn''t have noticed the ants. Could you have cropped it portrait and brought the focus in a little more on the bugs, but still have the feel and idea of the rose?', '2009-06-15 01:31:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(5528629, 796165, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First and foremost, I LOVE the story!  I had the same issue with lizards in Florida.  Other than a dropped tail, the lizard would be fine, and I''d have to rescue it and send it back outside.  <br/> <br/>The photograph - the mouse looks somewhat resigned to being saliva''d and carried about.  Surprisingly doesn''t look too terribly worried.  Cat looks quite pleased with herself!  Quality is somewhat snapshot-ish, but that''s due to the flash and the moving cat, and I''m sure you already know that.  Composition is tight - good job minimizing other stuff and keeping the attention on your subjects - but perhaps a little too tight and thay may be because of the aforementioned clutter reduction.  But above all else, a delightfully fun story to go with the photograph!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First and foremost, I LOVE the story!  I had the same issue with lizards in Florida.  Other than a dropped tail, the lizard would be fine, and I''d have to rescue it and send it back outside.   The photograph - the mouse looks somewhat resigned to being saliva''d and carried about.  Surprisingly doesn''t look too terribly worried.  Cat looks quite pleased with herself!  Quality is somewhat snapshot-ish, but that''s due to the flash and the moving cat, and I''m sure you already know that.  Composition is tight - good job minimizing other stuff and keeping the attention on your subjects - but perhaps a little too tight and thay may be because of the aforementioned clutter reduction.  But above all else, a delightfully fun story to go with the photograph!', '2009-06-15 04:13:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(5528630, 794295, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Hi Jorge, and welcome to DPC!  Darn fine looking model you have for this shot, and I like the monochromatic processing a lot!  For the challenge, I think it fits with the title you have, but as one commenter noted, it is title-dependent and some voters will score that poorly.  Had you had an actual nut in the photograph, it may have done better.  Challenge relevance needs to be very obvious to get high scores here.<br/> <br/>Technically, some may tell you not to have a centered composition, but this one works for me - it enhances that "in your face" feel that you have here with the direct eye contact with the squirrel.  Makes it almost agressive - and fun.  A bit dark on the right side - a slight adjustment with shadows/highlights (legal in basic) would bring out that side a bit.  Focus is good, clarity and sharpness pretty good as well.<br/> <br/>Look forward to seeing more entries from you!  If you have any questions, please feel free to send me a PM.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Hi Jorge, and welcome to DPC!  Darn fine looking model you have for this shot, and I like the monochromatic processing a lot!  For the challenge, I think it fits with the title you have, but as one commenter noted, it is title-dependent and some voters will score that poorly.  Had you had an actual nut in the photograph, it may have done better.  Challenge relevance needs to be very obvious to get high scores here. Technically, some may tell you not to have a centered composition, but this one works for me - it enhances that "in your face" feel that you have here with the direct eye contact with the squirrel.  Makes it almost agressive - and fun.  A bit dark on the right side - a slight adjustment with shadows/highlights (legal in basic) would bring out that side a bit.  Focus is good, clarity and sharpness pretty good as well. Look forward to seeing more entries from you!  If you have any questions, please feel free to send me a PM.', '2009-06-15 04:20:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5528632, 795733, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club! <br/> <br/>I''d have to agree with scooter''s comment. The connection to the challenge should be done with the picture not the title. It seemed there were a lot of images in this that were doing that so you aren''t alone.<br/> <br/>As for the shot itself: Great sharpness in the eyes. Colors are nicely saturated. The scene itself is really hard to shoot for however. There are some distracting bright spots and some areas that are just about completely blown out. Its a shame because the subject and the foreground are exposed nicely. <br/> <br/>Overall not a bad shot but one that might have done better in a different challenge.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club!  I''d have to agree with scooter''s comment. The connection to the challenge should be done with the picture not the title. It seemed there were a lot of images in this that were doing that so you aren''t alone. As for the shot itself: Great sharpness in the eyes. Colors are nicely saturated. The scene itself is really hard to shoot for however. There are some distracting bright spots and some areas that are just about completely blown out. Its a shame because the subject and the foreground are exposed nicely.  Overall not a bad shot but one that might have done better in a different challenge.', '2009-06-15 04:30:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529459, 794324, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>your comments, which you seem to have taken in stride, pretty much say it all.  compositionally, it''s a very complicated image with a lot going on.  the biggest issue, imho, is the lack of a single focal point.  while you''re trying to make a point about what can be considered an "enemy," you''re almost presenting the users with a bullet point list of other options.  it might have worked better to focus on one alternate theme and take your best shot at that.<br/> <br/>side note: to me it almost seemed like you were including credit cards in your enemies list, which is a little confusing in context of everything else.<br/> <br/>technically, shots with mirrors are very difficult to pull off well.  this might have been better on a plain background.  since the shot has so many subjects anyway, it makes sense to not muddy it any further with a complex background.<br/> <br/>good luck in future challenges!<br/>rob</td>', 'greetings from the critique club! your comments, which you seem to have taken in stride, pretty much say it all.  compositionally, it''s a very complicated image with a lot going on.  the biggest issue, imho, is the lack of a single focal point.  while you''re trying to make a point about what can be considered an "enemy," you''re almost presenting the users with a bullet point list of other options.  it might have worked better to focus on one alternate theme and take your best shot at that. side note: to me it almost seemed like you were including credit cards in your enemies list, which is a little confusing in context of everything else. technically, shots with mirrors are very difficult to pull off well.  this might have been better on a plain background.  since the shot has so many subjects anyway, it makes sense to not muddy it any further with a complex background. good luck in future challenges!rob', '2009-06-15 14:15:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529472, 796006, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>i like the concept for this challenge.  the shallow dof works well.  the poor lighting takes away from the image, however, and doesn''t provide much impact.<br/> <br/>compositionally, i think many users probably thought it was boring.  there''s not much going on, and it didn''t seem to take a lot of creativity or thought in it''s setup.  while i can see what you''re going for, in the end it''s just a couple of toys on a smudgy tabletop.  :(<br/> <br/>best of luck in the future.<br/>rob<br/></td>', 'greetings from the critique club! i like the concept for this challenge.  the shallow dof works well.  the poor lighting takes away from the image, however, and doesn''t provide much impact. compositionally, i think many users probably thought it was boring.  there''s not much going on, and it didn''t seem to take a lot of creativity or thought in it''s setup.  while i can see what you''re going for, in the end it''s just a couple of toys on a smudgy tabletop.  :( best of luck in the future.rob', '2009-06-15 14:22:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529480, 795995, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>i think this is a good idea for the challenge.  what killed you here is lighting.<br/> <br/>because you''re dividing the user''s attention to two subjects, you needed to have two light sources that made each subject pop.  like, for instance, if you had a boxing ring kind of feel, a spot on each can separated by some black space would have done it.  if you were going for a head-to-head kind of thing, you could light it with one really tight light that showed parts of each can basically posed against each other.<br/> <br/>in an image this dark, i don''t think the reflections would have had any impact.  it seemed like you were investing a lot in that effect when it really seems incidental.<br/> <br/>good luck in future challenges!<br/>rob</td>', 'greetings from the critique club! i think this is a good idea for the challenge.  what killed you here is lighting. because you''re dividing the user''s attention to two subjects, you needed to have two light sources that made each subject pop.  like, for instance, if you had a boxing ring kind of feel, a spot on each can separated by some black space would have done it.  if you were going for a head-to-head kind of thing, you could light it with one really tight light that showed parts of each can basically posed against each other. in an image this dark, i don''t think the reflections would have had any impact.  it seemed like you were investing a lot in that effect when it really seems incidental. good luck in future challenges!rob', '2009-06-15 14:27:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529497, 795945, 2170, '<td valign="top">greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>i think this is hysterical and i enjoy the image quite a bit.  my only suggestion for you would be to play with the angle you were shooting from.  even if you couldn''t see all of the three faces, i think this would have been fun to see almost straight on, with the barbed wire between the camera and the eggs.<br/> <br/>overall, excellent work!  good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>rob<br/></td>', 'greetings from the critique club! i think this is hysterical and i enjoy the image quite a bit.  my only suggestion for you would be to play with the angle you were shooting from.  even if you couldn''t see all of the three faces, i think this would have been fun to see almost straight on, with the barbed wire between the camera and the eggs. overall, excellent work!  good luck in future challenges! rob', '2009-06-15 14:32:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529709, 794932, 106979, '<td valign="top">can i donate money to you? because i rellay like this pictur, if you could get me one of a dumbell anda fishtank it wouold be marveklksous</td>', 'can i donate money to you? because i rellay like this pictur, if you could get me one of a dumbell anda fishtank it wouold be marveklksous', '2009-06-15 15:43:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529924, 796238, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is a hard subject to photograph. If you expose the glass properly then your background doesn''t have that nice white seamless look to it. I think the only way to overcome the problem is lighting the background and the subject. I''d be curious to see the lighting setup. <br/> <br/>The icecream / frozen dessert it a bit out of focus as well. I think that the fluid at the bottom is also distracting but that might be debatable. <br/> <br/>As for the challenge? In my opinion this was a shoe horn. Not as bad as some of the entries but it was a bit of a stretch and I think a lot of the voters might have agreed. I do understand the concept you were trying to convey so when I encountered this image during voting I didn''t deduct for not meeting the challenge. (I generally only do that when the shot clearly doesn''t meet the challenge).</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is a hard subject to photograph. If you expose the glass properly then your background doesn''t have that nice white seamless look to it. I think the only way to overcome the problem is lighting the background and the subject. I''d be curious to see the lighting setup.  The icecream / frozen dessert it a bit out of focus as well. I think that the fluid at the bottom is also distracting but that might be debatable.  As for the challenge? In my opinion this was a shoe horn. Not as bad as some of the entries but it was a bit of a stretch and I think a lot of the voters might have agreed. I do understand the concept you were trying to convey so when I encountered this image during voting I didn''t deduct for not meeting the challenge. (I generally only do that when the shot clearly doesn''t meet the challenge).', '2009-06-15 17:20:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(5529934, 795704, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>WOW, this is going to be a tough one to critique, especially without stepping into the realms of controversy.<br/> <br/>I found it very interesting to read peoples comments.  It seems a lot of people immediately jumped to conclusions about what you are trying to portray here.  This to me makes your representation all the more clever.  As I see it, all you are trying to show is that terrorists are typically hiding behind anonymity.  I see no reference to anything else. I think it says more about the commenters than anything else.<br/> <br/>I''m not surprised this didn''t break a 6, because there is an element of controversy, but it is a very strong image with a strong message.  Well done for a great job.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club WOW, this is going to be a tough one to critique, especially without stepping into the realms of controversy. I found it very interesting to read peoples comments.  It seems a lot of people immediately jumped to conclusions about what you are trying to portray here.  This to me makes your representation all the more clever.  As I see it, all you are trying to show is that terrorists are typically hiding behind anonymity.  I see no reference to anything else. I think it says more about the commenters than anything else. I''m not surprised this didn''t break a 6, because there is an element of controversy, but it is a very strong image with a strong message.  Well done for a great job. ', '2009-06-15 17:25:21.000000', NULL, 0),
